      "message": "Keep the door's promise\n\nThe operator's door was a list of paths. Underneath it, the unscoped\nadmin grant still satisfied every authority check on the public\nlistener, so an admin token that came through the tunnel could make\nany repository public, move it, rename it, set its policy or its\nmirror, bind a workload identity, or revoke anybody's credentials —\non routes that are an owner's the rest of the time. The grant now\ncounts only where the door is: the store is told per request which\nlistener a call came in on, and on the public one an admin's token is\nan owner's token and nothing more. The path list stays as the shape\nthe public listener answers in.\n\nAnd an invitation could hand an account over: inviting a name that\nalready existed put a new address on it and mailed a link that, when\nfollowed, proved that address and signed its holder in as that\naccount. With the console suggesting names from the local part of an\naddress, a stranger asking for an account as mandip@elsewhere would\nhave arrived ticked as mandip. An account anybody has been — signed\nin, proved an address, set a password — is refused a new address;\nthe address already on it may be sent a fresh link. A mail the forge\ncould not send also no longer takes the person off the waitlist.\n\nChange-Id: Idoor0112\nSigned-off-by: Mandip Adhikari <92096340+mandipadk@users.noreply.github.com>",
